      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Érd’s local history museum, housed in a 19th‑century house with a veranda, chronicles the town from pre‑historic times to the 1930s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://foldrajzimuzeum.hu/erd-local-history-exhibition/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name may derive from the Hungarian word for forest or from the word for stream.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the 1543 Ottoman capture, a motte castle and a mosque were built in Érd.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Érd boasts five train stations, including two in the center, making it a key commuter link to Budapest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The 17th‑century minaret in Érd is one of only three surviving Turkish minarets in Hungary.",
